{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_oj6lda6mvqzw4zrc7kglei4la2hni6mgxoxfqoan5d6ije2ihrnq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four gates pass.\n\n## What I found\n\n**One real correctness bug in the Rust translation**, plus one bug in my own test harness.\n\n### The library bug: `checkshift`'s allocation-failure branch vanished in release builds\n\nAt `-O2`, LLVM recognized the `malloc`/`free` pair in `checkshift` as non-escaping and **deleted the allocation outright** (heap-to-stack promotion). The release `.so` contained no `malloc` call at all inside `checkshift`, which made `if state.is_null()` dead code. Under allocation failure the C prints `Error: Failed to allocate memory for state` and returns `-1`, while optimized Rust sailed on and returned a normal result.\n\nThis was invisible in debug builds and invisible to any test that doesn't actually starve the allocator. Fixed by routing the `malloc` result through `core::hint::black_box`; confirmed via `objdump` that `malloc`/`free` survive, including under `lto=fat`, `codegen-units=1`, `opt-level=3`.\n\nI only caught it because I wrote E17 as a test that *executes* the branch rather than reasoning about it — an `LD_PRELOAD` shim (`tests/fixtures/failmalloc.c`) that fails `malloc(12)` inside an explicitly armed window around the single `checkshift` call, in a re-exec'd child.\n\n### The harness bug (worth flagging, since it nearly produced a false pass)\n\n`mutation_check.sh` restored pristine sources with `cp -a`, which preserves mtimes — so restored files looked *older* than the `.so` cargo had just built from mutated source, cargo skipped the rebuild, and a **mutated `.so` was left on disk**. The suite caught it (`Result of Xor:` vs `Result of XOR:`). Fixed, and `verify.sh` now defensively touches sources.\n\n## Two things I want to be explicit about\n\n**Everything passed on my first run** — 45/45 tests green. That's exactly when a differential suite deserves suspicion, so I mutation-tested it: 47 injected divergences, 47 caught, 0 blind spots. Two candidate mutations I deliberately excluded as *provably* equivalent rather than counting as misses:\n- `(checksum << 1)` → `rotate_left(1)`: at most 16 bytes are folded, so the accumulator never exceeds `0x007FFFFF` and bit 31 is never shifted out (verified numerically).\n- Reordering the short-circuit guard `!values.is_null() && count > 0`: both operands are side-effect free.\n\n**E17 was initially a structural test** (asserting on source text) — I flagged that as a real hole and replaced it with genuine execution, which is what surfaced the release-build bug. The structural version would have passed while the bug was live.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe crate has **no `[features]`**, so there is exactly one valid feature combination (the empty set) — derived mechanically, not assumed. Since a single combination can still hide profile-dependent bugs, I ran every phase in both `debug` and `release`; that's the only reason the bug was found.\n\nFinal state: 51 tests (6 harness self-checks, 26 Phase B, 19 Phase C), 26/26 `CONFIGS.md` rows, 25/25 `ERRORS.md` rows, symbol parity 0 missing / 0 undefined non-libc, all passing in both profiles, plus a 20 000-iteration-per-row soak. Comparison is byte-for-byte on return value *and* captured stdout. `c_src/` md5s are unchanged."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":938,"cache_read_input_tokens":166673,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":938,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b67cf027-de35-46ba-8757-83b8d3207f66","uuid":"f289c21e-a794-430e-abd2-f994535f11ef","timestamp":"2026-08-19T18:31:53.060Z"}
